      "side": 1,
      "message": "Interpolating the average from (665, 70C) and (500, 80C), I get:\nAdjusting 665 for scaling: 665 - (665 % 11) \u003d 660\nAdjusting 500 for scaling: 500 - (500 % 11) \u003d 495\nslope: 660-495/10 \u003d -16.5\n\nFor 75C, I calculate 577mV from the datasheet.\n\u003e\u003e\u003e x \u003d 6544.4; x / (x + 30900) * 3.3\n0.5767623463054554\n(665 - 577) / 16.5 + 70C \u003d 75.33degC \n\nFor 74C, I calculate 593mV from the datasheet.\n\u003e\u003e\u003e x \u003d 6775.5; x / (x + 30900) * 3.3\n0.5934665764223434\n(1815 - 593) / 16.5 \u003d 74.06degC\n\nI checked a few other numbers and the errors seemed really small. There\u0027s some error that comes up from integer math in the code as well, do engineers typically walk through every degree in the datasheet to look at this?\n\nI didn\u0027t go through the effort to see if I could remove entries from this table.\n\nThis is all using R-center values from the supplied data, which I assume is the right thing to do here.",
